{
  "notes": [
    "How every gun is SHOWN to its holder's own eyes while it is reloaded (FSOReloadShow, USOViewmodelComponent::ReloadPose).",
    "A show: [cm ahead of the eyes, share of the picture across, share down, muzzle up deg, muzzle in (left) deg, top rolled to the right deg].",
    "The place is where the FIRING hand holds the gun (the top of its grip); the gun is turned about that hand. The hand stays low, near the foot of the picture, and the gun is turned up about it: both forearms come to it from below with straight wrists (the owner's reference, 2026-11-27). A positive roll turns the left flank and the underside to the eyes and to the support hand (a magazine well, a loading port, a flap on the left); a negative one the top and the right flank (a handle on the right, a slide, a bolt).",
    "A style's 'show' is its base; 'anchors' are the shows for the places the support hand goes (fore, mag, pouch, spare, magbase, bolt, charge, slide, pump, cyl, ejector, loader, shell, round, hatch, biomass). A name begun with + adds to the base.",
    "'spring': the show's spring (omega, zeta), the jolts' (joltOmega, joltZeta), the share of the choreography's own keyed moves of the gun laid on the show (gunMoves), and handleLead: seconds the gun's turn runs AHEAD of the hand where the hand's next place is a handle or a slide (the gun is rolled right over for those: it is nearly round as the hand arrives).",
    "'beats': what each sound event of a reload does: 'move' the gun cm [right, up, back], 'turn' it deg [muzzle up, muzzle left, top to the left], 'view' deg [up, left, roll right]."
  ],
